 ## Overview
-Smoothstep Trilinear Interpolation is a technique used in multiresolution hash encodings to achieve $C^1$-smooth interpolation (continuous first derivatives) in the spatial domain [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of derivative discontinuities at grid boundaries inherent in standard trilinear interpolation, which can cause artifacts in neural rendering and gradient-based optimization [S1, unofficial][S2]. It sets itself apart from higher-order polynomial interpolations (like bicubic) by achieving similar smoothness properties at effectively zero computational cost, as the overhead is hidden by memory bottlenecks [S1, unofficial][S2]. It builds on [3D Trilinear Interpolation](2613-3d-trilinear-interpolation.md) by modifying the interpolation weights rather than the data structure or lookup count.
+Smoothstep Trilinear Interpolation achieves $C^1$-smooth interpolation (continuous first derivatives) in multiresolution hash encodings by applying a smoothstep function to standard trilinear interpolation weights [S1, unofficial][S2]. It eliminates derivative discontinuities at grid boundaries that cause artifacts in neural rendering and gradient-based optimization [S1, unofficial][S2]. It differs from higher-order polynomial interpolations like bicubic by achieving equivalent smoothness at effectively zero computational cost, as the arithmetic overhead is hidden by memory bottlenecks [S1, unofficial][S2]. It builds on [3D Trilinear Interpolation](2613-3d-trilinear-interpolation.md) by modifying the interpolation weights rather than the data structure or lookup count [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
