 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Zero Derivatives Alignment:** Without the level offset, the vanishing derivatives of the smoothstep at $x=0$ and $x=1$ cause discontinuities in the encoding's derivatives to persist due to the chain rule [S1, unofficial][S2]. The offset ensures that zero-derivative points do not align across all levels, allowing for non-zero derivatives at all spatial locations [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Higher-Order Quality Loss:** Using higher-order smoothstep functions ($S_n$ for $n > 1$) or higher-order polynomial interpolation (biquadratic/bicubic) reduces reconstruction quality [S1, unofficial][S2]. The reason for this quality loss is not fully explained and requires future research [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Computational Cost:** While the smoothstep function adds arithmetic operations, its cost is hidden by memory bottlenecks in typical GPU implementations, making it effectively free compared to the cost of additional memory lookups required by higher-order interpolation [S1, unofficial][S2].
