 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query per level, same as trilinear interpolation. The smoothstep function adds a constant number of arithmetic operations [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory overhead compared to standard trilinear interpolation. The hash table structure remains unchanged [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** In practice, the computational cost of the 1st order smoothstep function is hidden by memory bottlenecks, making it essentially free [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Achieves $C^1$-smoothness, eliminating derivative discontinuities at grid boundaries. Computationally efficient compared to higher-order polynomial interpolation.
-*   **Limitations:** Higher-order smoothstep functions ($n > 1$) reduce reconstruction quality [S1, unofficial][S2]. The quality loss mechanism is not fully understood [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When to Use:** When $C^1$-smoothness is required for neural rendering or gradient-based optimization, and computational efficiency is critical.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When maximum reconstruction quality is prioritized over smoothness, or when higher-order smoothness ($n > 1$) is needed (due to quality degradation).
