 ## Background
-Smoothstep Trilinear Interpolation was introduced in the context of Instant Neural Graphics Primitives, specifically for multiresolution hash encodings [S1, unofficial][S2]. The method was proposed as a low-cost alternative to $d$-quadratic or $d$-cubic interpolation, which are expensive due to the increased number of vertex lookups [S1, unofficial][S2]. The technique leverages the properties of the smoothstep function to achieve $C^1$-smoothness without significant computational overhead [S1, unofficial][S2]. The offsetting of resolution levels was introduced to prevent the alignment of zero-derivative points across levels, ensuring non-zero derivatives everywhere [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Smoothstep Trilinear Interpolation was introduced in the context of Instant Neural Graphics Primitives for multiresolution hash encodings [S1, unofficial][S2]. The method was proposed as a low-cost alternative to $d$-quadratic or $d$-cubic interpolation, which are computationally expensive due to requiring $3^d$ and $4^d$ vertex lookups respectively [S1, unofficial][S2]. The technique leverages the vanishing derivative property of the smoothstep function to achieve $C^1$-smoothness without significant computational overhead [S1, unofficial][S2]. The offsetting of resolution levels was introduced to prevent the alignment of zero-derivative points across levels, ensuring non-zero derivatives everywhere [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
